PROC SORT ... NODUPKEY keeps the first row for each BY-group after sorting.

Your SAS code:

proc sort data=JR.center_prev_6_&t_1_m nodupkey;
by centerStaffID CenterId descending Month;
run;


means:

Sort by:
centerStaffID ascending
CenterId ascending
Month descending
For each (centerStaffID, CenterId) combination, keep only the latest Month record.
PySpark equivalent
from pyspark.sql import functions as F
from pyspark.sql.window import Window

w = Window.partitionBy(
    "centerStaffID",
    "CenterId"
).orderBy(
    F.col("Month").desc()
)

result = (
    df
    .withColumn("rn", F.row_number().over(w))
    .filter(F.col("rn") == 1)
    .drop("rn")
)

Example

Input:

+-------------+--------+----------+
|centerStaffID|CenterId|Month     |
+-------------+--------+----------+
|1001         |A       |2026-03-01|
|1001         |A       |2026-05-01|
|1001         |A       |2026-04-01|
|1002         |B       |2026-02-01|
|1002         |B       |2026-06-01|
+-------------+--------+----------+


Output:

+-------------+--------+----------+
|centerStaffID|CenterId|Month     |
+-------------+--------+----------+
|1001         |A       |2026-05-01|
|1002         |B       |2026-06-01|
+-------------+--------+----------+

Why dropDuplicates() is not enough

This:

df.dropDuplicates(["centerStaffID", "CenterId"])


is not equivalent because Spark can keep any row from each group.

The SAS code explicitly sorts by Month DESC first and then keeps the first record, so the correct PySpark translation is the row_number() window approach above.
